          %p
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+square"} exact
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝕀
                    %mi o
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{I}_o
            is the category of objects and horizontal morphisms of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕀
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{I}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝕀
                    %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{I}_a
            is the category of arrows and 2-cells.
          %p The situation for cocompleteness is dual, replacing lax by colax everywhere.
          %p
            For more on this see
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#GrandisParé07"} Grandis and Paré
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The above considerations show that any Grothendieck fibration
                %em or
                opfibration is a (strict) Conduché functor, while any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Street+fibration"} Street fibration
                or opfibration is a non-strict Conduché functor.
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{2}
                denotes the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+category"} interval category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+lax+functor"} normal lax functor
                out of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{2}
                is necessarily pseudo, since there are no composable pairs of nonidentity arrows in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{2}
                %em any
                functor with codomain
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{2}
                is a Conduché functor. Note that functors with codomain
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{2}
                can also be identified with
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctors"} profunctors
            %li
              %p
                As with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponentiable+morphisms"} exponentiable morphisms
                in any category, Conduché functors are closed under composition.
          %h2#references References
          %p That Conduché functors are exactly the exponentiable ones is Theorem 4.4 (pages 40-45) of
          %ul
            %li#Giraud64
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J.+Giraud"}> J. Giraud
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Méthode de la descente
              %strong 2
              (1964). (
              %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/numdam-bin/item?id=MSMF_1964__2__R3_0"}> numdam
              )
          %p See also,
          %ul
            %li#Conduche72
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F.+Conduch%C3%A9"}> F. Conduché
                ,
                %em Au sujet de l’existence d’adjoints à droite aux foncteurs ‘image reciproque’ dans la catégorie des catégories
                , C. R. Acad. Sci. Paris
                %strong 275
                Série A (1972) pp.891-894. (
                %a{:href => "http://gallica.bnf.fr/ark:/12148/bpt6k56191352/f19.image#"}> gallica
                )
            %li{:id => "GrandisParé07"}
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marco+Grandis"}> Marco Grandis
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Par%C3%A9"} Robert Paré
                %em Lax Kan extensions for double categories (on weak double categories, part IV)
                , Cahiers de topologie et géométrie différentielle catégoriques, tome 48, no 3 (2007), p. 163-199
          %p Some of definitions and proofs of the above theorems, along with the 2-categorical generalization (Conduché considered only the 1-categorical case) can also be found in (e.g. see Lemma 6.1 for a proof that Conduché implies exponentiability):
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
              , “Fibrations and partial products in a 2-category”,
              %em Appl. Categ. Structures
              1 (1993), 141–179
          %p A description of the characterization in terms of lax normal functors can be found in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"}> Ross Street
              , 'Powerful functors',
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.mq.edu.au/~street/Pow.fun.pdf"} pdf
          %p Discrete Conduché functors are considered in
          %ul
            %li#BN00
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marta+Bunge"}> M. Bunge
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Susan+Niefield"} S. Niefield
                %em Exponentiability and single universes
                , JPAA
                %strong 148
                (2000) pp.217-250.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                ,
                %em A Note on Discrete Conduché Fibrations
                , TAC
                %strong 5
                no.1 (1999) pp.1-11. (
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/1999/n1/n1.pdf"}> pdf
                )
          %p
            An analogue of Conduché functors for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-categories"} ∞-categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Prof"} Prof
          %ul
            %li#AyalaFrancis
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Ayala"} David Ayala
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Francis"} John Francis
              = succeed "," do
                %em Fibrations of ∞-categories
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.02681"} arxiv
          %p A discussion of ULF functors (including the fact they form a factorisation system) is contained in:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2010/08/pullbackhomomorphisms.html"}> Pullback-homomorphisms
                , n-Category Café (2010)
            %li
              %p
